      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 ASCPT.",
        "AbstractText": "Opioids are controversial in the treatment of chronic pain due to the risk of addiction, misuse, and death. Long-acting formulations of opioids have been hypothesized to improve pain relief, sleep quality, and lower the risk of side effects and addiction compared to short-acting formulations of opioids. Thus, several guidelines have recommended the use of long-acting opioid formulations over short-acting ones in the treatment of chronic noncancer pain. However, is there really evidence of their superiority?"
      },
